## ConfigMaps: data vs dataMap

`configMap.configs` creates one ConfigMap per entry, named `<appName>-<nameSuffix>`.
Each config sets exactly one of two forms:

|  | `data` | `dataMap` |
|---|---|---|
| Shape | one multiline string | key/value map of flat scalars |
| Helm templating (`tpl`) | yes | no — values render literally |
| Override across values files | replaced as a whole | keys merge individually |
| Delete a key from an overlay | not possible | set the key to `null` |

Use `dataMap` for per-environment overrides: an env overlay changes one key while the
base file keeps the rest. Setting a whole config to `null` in an overlay removes that
ConfigMap. Null-deletion works when all values files are passed together (as ArgoCD
does); it does **not** work through `helm upgrade --reuse-values`, which silently keeps
the old key.

### Quote anything YAML could type

Helm parses values files as YAML 1.1 *before* the chart can validate anything, so
unquoted boolean words (`y`, `n`, `yes`, `no`, `true`, `false`, `on`, `off` — any case)
and numbers are coerced silently: `on: x` becomes the key `"true"`; `no:` and `off:`
both collapse to `"false"` and one value is lost; `1.10` becomes `"1.1"`; `0755`
becomes `"493"`. When in doubt, quote both key and value. The comment block under
`configMap.configs` in values.yaml lists every coercion.

The chart enforces the key side of this rule: a `dataMap` key arriving as literal
`true` or `false` fails the render, because it is almost always a coerced unquoted
boolean and the `no:`/`off:` collision silently loses a value. Keys genuinely named
`true`/`false` are not supported (post-parse they are indistinguishable from the
accident). Value coercion (`1.10` → `"1.1"`) cannot be detected after parsing —
quoting is the only protection there.

### Migrating a config from data to dataMap

**Pre-flight: search the `data` string for `{{ "{{" }}` before converting.** `data` is
rendered through `tpl`, `dataMap` is not — a converted `{{ "{{ .Values.appName }}" }}`
renders as literal braces and ships to the app with no error at any layer (`helm
template` and `helm lint` both exit 0). Keep templated configs on `data`.

If only some keys are templated, split them into a second config — each config renders
its own ConfigMap, so the templated keys stay on `data` while the rest gain per-key
merging. The cost: the second ConfigMap has a **different name** (`<appName>-<suffix>`),
so every `volumeMounts`, `envFrom` and `envConfigMaps` reference to the original must be
updated to list both names.

Mixing the two forms for one config is an error in both directions, so migrate one
config across the base file **and every environment overlay in the same commit**:

```yaml
# BEFORE
# --- base-values.yaml
configMap:
  configs:
    app:
      data: |-
        API_PORT: "8080"
        LOG_LEVEL: info
# --- values-<env>.yaml
configMap:
  configs:
    app:
      data: |-
        API_PORT: "8080"
        LOG_LEVEL: debug
```

```yaml
# AFTER
# --- base-values.yaml
configMap:
  configs:
    app:
      dataMap:
        API_PORT: "8080"
        LOG_LEVEL: info
# --- values-<env>.yaml
configMap:
  configs:
    app:
      dataMap:
        LOG_LEVEL: debug   # only the override
```

Other guardrails, all failing at template time with an error naming the config:
values must be flat scalars (string/number/bool), keys must match `[-._a-zA-Z0-9]+`,
the rendered name must be a valid RFC 1123 subdomain, and content is text-only
(`binaryData` is not supported). The apiserver's 1 MiB per-ConfigMap limit still
applies and is not checked at template time — an oversized ConfigMap renders clean and
fails on apply.

Changing a `dataMap` key does **not** restart pods: no template emits a config
checksum annotation, so config consumed through `envFrom`/`envConfigMaps` stays stale
until the next restart, and deleting a key that is still referenced surfaces as
`CreateContainerConfigError` only when a container next starts. Roll the workload
yourself after a config-only change.

### Upgrading from 0.14.x

`configMap.configs` is validated at template time from 0.15.0 onwards. Shapes that
previously rendered (or rendered *wrongly*) now fail the render with an error naming
the config, so a bad config stops the sync instead of shipping. Check for these before
upgrading:

- A config that sets both `data` and `dataMap` — previously the stray `dataMap` was
  ignored, now it is a hard error. Grep your values files for configs carrying both.
- A config that sets neither, or sets `data` to a non-string (map/list/number), or
  `configs` itself to something other than a map — these failed before too, but with
  different (Go-internal) error text, so any tooling grepping for the old strings needs
  updating.
- A `nameSuffix` whose rendered ConfigMap name (`<appName>-<suffix>`) is not a valid
  RFC 1123 subdomain — previously accepted at template time and rejected at apply time.

Existing string `data` configs are otherwise unaffected and render byte-identically to
0.14.x. New in 0.15.0: the `dataMap` form, per-key merging across values files, and
`null`-deletion of individual keys.

## Upgrading from 0.16.x

From 0.17.0, pod templates (Deployment, StatefulSet, Job and CronJob
`spec.template.metadata`) no longer carry the `helm.sh/chart` label and annotation.
Top-level resources still do. The value is `<chart>-<version>`, so it changed every pod
template on every chart release: all Deployments and StatefulSets rolled on each chart
bump, and existing Jobs failed with `spec.template: field is immutable`.

Removing it changes every pod template once. Before upgrading, check:

- **One rollout.** Every Deployment and StatefulSet rolls once; apps show `Progressing`
  until it finishes. Single-replica StatefulSets are briefly unavailable. Single-replica
  Deployments stay available with the default `RollingUpdate` strategy (the chart renders
  `maxSurge: 100%` / `maxUnavailable: 0`), provided they have readiness probes. Roll out
  to tenants in stages, canary first (e.g. ApplicationSet progressive sync, or pause
  auto-sync).
- **Existing Jobs.** A Job that has started or finished fails this one upgrade with
  `spec.template: field is immutable`. Suspended, never-started Jobs, Jobs already
  removed by `ttlSecondsAfterFinished`, and Argo CD hook Jobs are unaffected.
  - Argo CD (default client-side diff): the sync reports `Failed`, the other resources
    still apply, and auto-sync retries until fixed.
  - Argo CD with ServerSideDiff (including `ServerSideApply=true` setups using it): sync
    status shows `Unknown` ("Failed to compare desired state to live state") and
    **nothing** syncs, including the Deployment, until the Job is gone.
  - Plain `helm upgrade`: the release is left `failed`, with the other resources applied.

  **Fix: delete those Jobs.** They are recreated and run again, so make sure that is
  safe (e.g. an idempotent migration). `Replace=true` alone does not work. With Argo CD's
  default client-side diff only, you can instead set
  `argocd.argoproj.io/sync-options: Replace=true,Force=true` through `job.annotations`
  (rendered only on the Job's own metadata), never app-wide (app-wide
  `Replace=true,Force=true` recreates every resource: an outage). It has no effect with
  ServerSideDiff, and it deletes and re-runs the Job on every sync that changes it, so
  remove it after the upgrade. Do not use `job.jobs.<name>.annotations` for this: those are
  also rendered on the pod template, so removing them later changes `spec.template`
  again.
- **Anything using the pod's `helm.sh/chart` label.** Pods, ReplicaSets and
  ControllerRevisions lose it. Switch to `app.kubernetes.io/name` /
  `app.kubernetes.io/instance` in NetworkPolicies, PodMonitors, `kubectl -l`, kube-state-metrics
  `kube_pod_labels{label_helm_sh_chart}` (when allowlisted), and Prometheus / Alloy /
  Pyroscope `labelmap` rules over pod labels. Datadog: the KSM core check derives a
  `helm_chart` tag from this label, so `kubernetes_state.pod.*`, `kubernetes_state.container.*`
  and ReplicaSet metrics lose `helm_chart` (Deployment / StatefulSet / Job / CronJob
  metrics and the Datadog Helm check keep it). Move monitors to `kube_app_name` /
  `kube_app_instance` or `kube_deployment` / `kube_stateful_set`.
- **New pod annotation `glueops.dev/chart: app`.** Constant (chart name, never the
  version). It keeps the pod-template annotations non-empty: with an empty map, Argo CD's
  default diff reports annotations added outside the chart (`kubectl rollout restart`,
  Argo CD "Restart", Stakater Reloader) as permanently `OutOfSync`. A `commonAnnotations`
  entry with the same key overrides it.
- **Custom resources.** Pod templates you write in `customResources` /
  `customResourcesMap` that include `chart.commonLabels` / `chart.commonAnnotations`
  still get `helm.sh/chart`. Use `chart.podTemplateLabels` / `chart.podTemplateAnnotations`
  there.

After this upgrade, chart-only version bumps no longer restart pods, provided:

- `appVersion` is set to a non-empty value and never changed after install. Empty, it
  falls back to the chart version in `app.kubernetes.io/version`, which is also part of
  the immutable selectors.
- The image tag does not come from the chart version. The tag resolves `image.tag` →
  `appVersion` → chart version (with `image.useChartVersionAsTagFallback: true`, the
  default), so set `image.tag` or a non-empty `appVersion`.

